# Context window management

## A window is not a bag, it is a spotlight

A window is not storage with a limit; it is a fixed amount of attention. Three
things follow, and they contradict each other, so they are worth stating
separately.

1. **Position matters.** Instructions and information at the very start and very
   end of a window are attended to most. The same material in the middle is
   attended to least. This is the single most reliable, least used fact about
   long contexts.
2. **Retrieval is lossy.** Pulling the right twenty lines out of a hundred
   thousand is a probabilistic operation, not a database lookup.
3. **Coherence degrades before the cap.** You will start contradicting yourself
   long before you run out of tokens. The working size is well under the hard
   limit.

So "I have plenty of context left" is not the same as "I can still do this
correctly", and the gap widens with task length.

## Structure the window by phase

Do not run one continuous conversation and hope. Give each phase its own framing,
so the material for the current phase is salient and the rest is not competing.

```
[ invariants ]   the constraints that never change: conventions, constraints,
                 what must not change. Small, and repeated.

[ decisions ]   settled so far, with the reason. This is what compaction must
                 not lose.

[ current ]     the task in front of you, the files in play, the open question.

[ next ]        what you are about to do, stated concretely.
```

`next` at the end matters more than it looks. A window that ends on "so, about
the migration…" resumes far better than one that ends on a wall of evidence.

## Compaction drops reasoning and keeps conclusions

This is the asymmetry to design around. When a window is summarized, what
survives is roughly: what was concluded, what files were touched, what remains
open. What is lost is **the chain of reasoning that justified the conclusion**.

The practical consequence: a conclusion carried across compaction is an
assertion with no visible justification, and it will be re-litigated or, worse,
trusted without checking. So every decision worth surviving must carry its
reason with it, in a form that survives:

```
- Chose polling over SSE. Why: deployment target has no sticky routing, and SSE
  needs it. Revisit if: routing config changes.
```

Without the `Why:` clause this is a verdict with no visible reasoning, and the
next context has to re-derive it.

## Make compaction a non-event

Everything below is about the same idea: if the durable state is outside the
window, losing the window costs nothing.

- **Decisions and rejections in a file**, not in the conversation. See
  `token-and-context-budget` for the shape that works.
- **File paths and line numbers in prose**, so the next context does not re-search
  for what it already found.
- **Open questions stated as questions.** An open question is easy to resume; a
  half-finished sentence is not.
- **Verification results recorded** — "the suite passed at abc123", not "tests
  pass", which is a claim the next context must re-establish.

## Chunk long work deliberately

A task that exceeds the window is not a task to attempt in one pass. Break it so
that each chunk ends in a **committed state** rather than a partial one.

The wrong chunking is by file or by topic: you read three of the eight modules,
run out, and come back with none of them integrated.

The right chunking is by **outcome**. Each pass finishes something checkable:
one module changed and tested, one hypothesis tested, one migration written. Then
the next pass starts from a state that is true rather than a state that is
half-applied.

## Position deliberately when you must hold a lot

When the task genuinely requires breadth, order matters more than volume.

- Put **invariants first**, so they are the most-attended material when a
  judgement call comes up at the end.
- Put **the current question last**, so it is the most recent thing in the window.
- Put **evidence near the decision it supports**, not gathered in a block at the
  top. Evidence separated from its claim decays and gets re-read.
- Put **contradictions next to each other.** Two sources that disagree, placed
  together, get resolved. Placed apart, one wins by recency and nobody notices.

## Retrieve, do not dump

Indexing your own context beats holding it. When the material is a large
codebase, a set of documents, or a long log:

1. **Build a compact index** — the file, what it is for, and one line on its
  shape. Hundreds of entries, a few thousand tokens.
2. **Query the index**, then open only what the answer names.
3. **Keep the index in a file** so the next phase inherits it.

The same shape serves long sessions: a table of what exists, a table of what was
tried, a table of what is decided. Each is small, each is durable, and together
they replace the narrative the window would otherwise have to carry.

## Know when to restart

Sometimes the right move is to abandon the window and start from the durable
state. A session that has contradicted itself twice, or that is three phases into
a task whose premises have changed, is producing worse work than a fresh start
with a good notes file.

The test is not length. It is whether you still trust the earlier material. When
you would not act on your own conclusions from an hour ago without re-reading
them, the window has stopped working for you.

## Anti-rationalisations

| Excuse | Reality |
| --- | --- |
| "I still have plenty of context." | Coherence degrades long before the cap. Length is not capacity. |
| "I will summarise the decision." | Compaction keeps conclusions and drops reasons. Carry the `Why:`. |
| "Notes in context are fine." | They are in the thing that gets discarded. Put them in a file. |
| "Position does not matter." | It matters more than volume does. End on the current question. |
| "One continuous session is simpler." | It is simpler until it contradicts itself, then it is worse than nothing. |
| "I will just read everything again." | That is the cost you were trying to avoid. Build the index instead. |
| "Restarting loses work." | It loses what was in the window, which is the part you cannot verify anyway. |
